  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 251 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: The center of the low associated with the remnants of \nIsaias will track up into the Mid-Atlantic then track offshore \nbefore it reaches any farther north. Rainfall will spread into \nsouthern areas this evening but struggle to make it any farther \nnorth as it is running into the ~1030 MB high that is giving us the \ndry weekend. The 00Z guidance has trended south with the northern \nextent of the rain, with the global deterministic data showing the \nmost dramatic retreat while the HREF and global ensembles show a \nmore subtle retreat. Overall, areas with a 50 percent or higher \nchance of seeing 0.1 inches is now mostly restricted to southern \nVermont, with measurable rain likely not reaching the international \nborder in places. Forecast rain amounts range from close to a half \ninch in southern Windsor/Rutland counties to nothing along much of \nQuebec border.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A surface low will pass through from west to east \nTuesday night, bringing widespread showers. While it will be quick \nmoving and technically more northern stream and not have a strong \nconnection to Gulf/Atlantic moisture, it will include the remnants \nof Tropical Storm Rachael so there will be some efficient rainfall \nwith the former tropical moisture. GEFS/EPS/CMCE combined \nprobabilities of a half inch of rain range between 40-70 percent and \nprobabilities of an inch are between 10-30 percent, so the rain \nwould only be beneficial. The heaviest rain should fall in northern \nareas. Warm air aloft will remain on the backside so even though the \ncold front drops temperatures significantly, any upslope showers \nlook to remain rain in the mountains. Conditions trend drier for \nWednesday, but under a classic cyclonic northwest flow cold air \nadvection regime, there will be some upslope sprinkles and showers \nbut no widespread rain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: A dynamic low pressure system brings winds and rain to \nthe region for Friday into Saturday. Showers will linger behind this \nsystem as upper level trough remains over the region. Still a lot of \nuncertainty about this system since it is six days away. Models are \nstill not in very good agreement in this timeframe either. GFS has a \ndeeper and stronger low while the ECMWF depicts more of an open wave \nas the system moves through.",
